OpenBSD fetch C regressions

From the repository root:
    make
    make regress

From this directory:
    make regress
    ./fetch-regress -l
    ./fetch-regress Chunk SOCKS5
    FETCH_BIN=/absolute/path/to/fetch ./fetch-regress Retry

Arguments select test names by substring (OR). No match is an error.
The default executable is ../../fetch; FETCH_BIN overrides it. Build and
run from this directory. make regress regenerates short-lived local TLS
certificates with base openssl. Direct runner invocation needs those
certificates from an earlier make regress or sh certificates.sh.

Requirements: OpenBSD base cc, make, libc, libtls and openssl. ktrace/kdump
are used for the unveil test. No Go, Python, external web service, or
third-party test framework is required. The .invalid DNS failure test
uses the host resolver; all protocol servers listen on loopback.

Each named case runs in its own process group and private directory.
Fixtures bind ephemeral listeners before clients run. Fetch inherits a
clean environment and no fixture descriptors. Binary output is compared
by length and bytes. Fetch has a 15-second harness watchdog; fixtures have
25 seconds and each case 90 seconds. These ceilings are distinct from
fetch's -T timeout assertions. Interrupted/failing cases kill fixtures;
completed fixtures with abnormal status fail the case. A signal is never
accepted as an ordinary fetch failure.

PASS, FAIL and SKIP are printed separately. Root skips checks that require
permission denial. Special-bit setup may be denied for an ordinary user.
Unavailable IPv6 or tracing support is reported as a skip where applicable.
Run under the intended deployment user; a pass is not a claim of security.

coverage.tsv lists each case with the assertions and parameter sets it covers.
Existing tests/tls.sh and tests/sandbox.sh remain separate; inspect their
setup and external-service requirements before running them.
